Redact the user's home directory from module paths in minidumps.

The module list names every loaded module by full path, and on a developer or
end-user machine most of those paths sit under the account's home directory, so
an uploaded minidump identifies the local user. Crashpad's handler writes the
dump in its own process and uploads it directly, so there is no client-side
sentry-native hook that could rewrite it -- the redaction has to happen here.

The component following a path component named "Users" or "home" is replaced
with "<user>". Both / and \ count as separators, so one implementation covers
macOS, Linux and Windows with no conditional compilation. Paths outside a home
directory, such as /System, /usr/lib and C:\Windows, are left alone, and the
rest of the path is kept so a report still shows where a module was loaded from.

Two writers emit module names and both are redacted:

  MinidumpModuleWriter::SetName            the module list
  MinidumpUnloadedModuleWriter::SetName    the unloaded module list, which
                                           crashpad populates on Windows

Redacting inside SetName rather than at the InitializeFromSnapshot call sites
covers every caller. Symbolication is unaffected: Sentry matches debug files by
debug ID, and the CodeView records already carry bare basenames.

tools/check-minidump.py asserts the result: it fails on any home directory path
in a dump whose user component is not the placeholder.

Applies to sentry-native's external/crashpad submodule; see tools/buildsentry.sh
and ci/x64-windows/sentry.bat.

